Java stimulator 

Introduction
This is a Java program that simulates a simple STC89C52/8051-style CPU. It helps students understand how a CPU runs assembly instructions.

Main Features
Simulates registers, memory and basic flags.
Runs simple assembly-like instructions.
Supports step-by-step and full execution.
Shows the Fetch–Decode–Execute process.

Main Parts
CPU stores registers and flags. Memory stores program and data. Instruction and InstructionSet handle commands. Simulator controls the whole program

Registers
A, B and R0–R7 are 8-bit registers. PC stores the next instruction. SP is the stack pointer. CY is the carry flag and Z shows whether A is zero.

How It Works
FETCH → DECODE → EXECUTE
The simulator gets an instruction, understands it, executes it, and then moves to the next instruction.

Instructions
MOV – move data
XCH – swap values
ADD / SUBB – arithmetic
ANL / ORL – logic operations
INC / DEC – increase or decrease
SJMP – jump
CLR – clear A
END – stop the program

Example
MOV A,#05
MOV R0,#03
XCH A,R0
ADD A,#03
END
The simulator changes the CPU values as each instruction runs.

Testing
The project has tests for the main instructions and a complete demo program. Example: MOV A,#05 followed by ADD A,#03 should give A = 08.

Limitations
This is only a basic educational simulator. It does not fully reproduce real STC89C52 hardware, I/O, timers, interrupts or all 8051 
instructions.

Conclusion
The project gives a simple introduction to CPU registers, memory and instruction execution.
